description:
On the switch bar, by the coordinator's ruling of 2026-10-28: the owner sends while an agent works. Without this he would have to wait for idle before sending.

Parity target is Qt's composer flow while the agent is busy: the native queue (queued messages, their order, edit and remove) and Send and push now (tray/src/MessageComposer.cpp, HgsClient.cpp, SessionsWindow.cpp), using the hgs CLI's queue and push-now paths.

The existing send rules still apply:
- the durable sending save happens before dispatch;
- an Uncertain outcome is never resent automatically;
- request ids are reused only for the same submission.

Split:
- the core (the events session) owns the queue model and the guarded send variants;
- webui owns the composer controls and the queue view.

Planned as phase 4 Part A2.

On the switch bar, by the coordinator's ruling of 2026-10-28: the owner answers Claude, Codex and Kimi question cards and approvals from the app every day. Without this, after the switch every question would have to be answered in the Terminal tab.

Parity target is the Qt flow:
- the question and approval cards in Activity and the composer (tray/src/ActivityView.cpp, MessageComposer.cpp, SessionsWindow.cpp);
- the answer path through `hgs answer` (HgsClient.cpp), with its identity and guard rules.

AGENTS rules apply:
- unknown delivery never authorizes replay;
- an approval needs a verified one-time native permission and keeps the exact run, conversation and question identity;
- broader options always go through the verified screen with a second Confirm.

Split:
- the core (the events session) owns parsing the question and approval events and the guarded answer action;
- webui owns the cards, the commands and the dialogs.

Planned as phase 4 Part A2.

Option, not scheduled (owner 2026-10-22: file it, do not implement yet).

Problem: the daily Agent upstream canary (.github/workflows/agent-upstream.yml, scripts/ci/agent-compatibility.py --latest) already discovers Codex, Claude Code, OpenCode, Kimi Code and the DeepSeek Harness. discover_upstream marks a provider 'needs review' when its latest is not in reviewed_versions of scripts/ci/agent-compatibility.json, but that only reaches the run summary and summary.md, so new releases go unnoticed: dsh 0.2.1-rc.2 (2026-10-17) waited five days until an owner request (adopted in PR #298). A Beads memory reminding every session was rejected as overkill; Dependabot does not fit (monthly cadence, would bump only the fixture package.json, unreliable with prereleases).

Proposal: after discovery, the scheduled run maintains a single GitHub issue, for example 'Native agent releases need review', labelled agent-upstream:
- create it when at least one provider is 'needs review' (OpenCode 'newer than the reviewed minimum' counts only if its canary failed);
- update the body in place with provider, latest version, reviewed versions, release-notes link and the run link; never open a second issue (find by label, newest open);
- close it with a final comment when every provider is reviewed again;
- report discovery errors (registry unreachable) separately from 'needs review', and never react to qualification test failures, which stay the canary's own red status (e.g. zerus-8rfj).

Security/permissions: only the scheduled run on refs/heads/main of ufna/zerus, never PRs or forks; issues: write granted to a separate small job that runs after the canary, checks out nothing it executes beyond the reviewed script or reads the summary artifact, and has no other secrets or cache access. Pin any action by SHA. Extend tests/test_ci_security.py to assert the permission is confined to that job and the trigger guard.

Acceptance: a dry-run mode or unit test of the create/update/close decision from discovery rows; docs/ci-and-aur.md native compatibility section describes the issue; adopting a release still means updating reviewed_versions (and pins) in a reviewed PR.
